<Project Sdk="Microsoft.NET.Sdk">

  <!--
    The DA5 round-trip tests: every procedure is CALLED, against a real SQL Server, and its result is
    materialised and checked. The offline unit tests live in the sibling project whose name ends
    .Data.Tests; these are a separate assembly because they WRITE and this database has no hard
    delete. Every row they write is permanent, so running them has to be a decision rather than a
    side effect of `dotnet test`.

    (That sibling is not named in full here on purpose. build/check_cross_database.py reads any
    three-part dotted name after the word "from" as a SQL object reaching outside this database,
    and it is right to: a rule that exempted prose would exempt a comment above the reference too.)

    That decision is the RCRAINFO_INTEGRATION environment variable. Without it every test here skips;
    with it, an unreachable server is a FAILURE rather than a skip, so a green run cannot mean the
    suite quietly examined nothing. See IntegrationServer.

    The name ends in '.Tests' on purpose. Directory.Build.props keys the test-project settings off
    exactly that suffix, and '.IntegrationTests' would not match it.
  -->

  <PropertyGroup>
    <IsPackable>false</IsPackable>
    <IsTestProject>true</IsTestProject>
  </PropertyGroup>

  <ItemGroup>
    <PackageReference Include="coverlet.collector" />
    <PackageReference Include="Microsoft.NET.Test.Sdk" />
    <PackageReference Include="xunit" />
    <PackageReference Include="xunit.runner.visualstudio" />
  </ItemGroup>

  <ItemGroup>
    <ProjectReference Include="..\..\src\RCRAInfo.Data\RCRAInfo.Data.csproj" />
  </ItemGroup>

  <ItemGroup>
    <!--
      One implementation of repository-root discovery, shared by source rather than by copy. TestPaths
      is internal, so linking the file compiles it into this assembly; a second copy would be a second
      thing to keep correct.

      The generated payload fixture is deliberately NOT copied to the output directory. It is read from
      the working tree for the reason TestPaths itself gives: a copy is a thing that can be stale, and a
      stale fixture would make these tests agree with a spec nobody is using any more.
    -->
    <Compile Include="..\RCRAInfo.Data.Tests\TestPaths.cs" Link="TestPaths.cs" />
  </ItemGroup>

  <ItemGroup>
    <Using Include="Xunit" />
  </ItemGroup>

</Project>
